- Connaissance d'une langue locale (Gbaya, Fulfulde, Sango, etc…) est un atout.

**5. REGLEMENTATIONS GENERALES**

- L’employé doit respecter les instructions et politiques de DRC en matière de sécurité, de confidentialité et d’éthique, y compris le Code de conduite et le cadre de responsabilité humanitaire ;
- L’employé ne doit exercer aucune autre activité rémunérée pendant la durée de son contrat avec DRC sans autorisation préalable, conformément aux politiques applicables ;
- L’employé ne doit s’engager dans aucune activité susceptible de nuire à DRC ou à la bonne mise en œuvre des projets pendant la durée de son contrat ;
- L’employé ne doit accorder d’interviews aux médias, publier des photos liées aux programmes de DRC ou partager des documents appartenant à DRC sans autorisation préalable ;
- L’employé doit restituer tout équipement mis à sa disposition par DRC pour l’exercice de ses fonctions à la fin de son contrat ou à la demande de DRC.

**6. COMPETENCES ET QUALITES EXIGEES**

À ce poste, le/la titulaire doit démontrer les cinq compétences essentielles de DRC :

- **Viser l’excellence :** se concentrer sur l’obtention de résultats tout en garantissant un processus efficace. S’efforcer de produire un travail précis, approfondi et professionnel, avec une utilisation optimale du temps et des ressources ;
- **Collaborer :** coopérer avec les parties concernées et les impliquer dans les processus pertinents, rechercher activement leur contribution et partager les informations clés. Soutenir les autres et favoriser un environnement propice au feedback ;
- **Prendre les rênes :** assumer la responsabilité de son travail et le prioriser en fonction de la vision et des objectifs généraux de DRC. Prendre des initiatives face aux défis et rechercher des solutions appropriées et innovantes ;
- **Communiquer :** communiquer de manière efficace, honnête et adaptée au contexte, tant à l’oral qu’à l’écrit. Pratiquer l’écoute active et favoriser le dialogue ;
- **Faire preuve d’intégrité :** respecter et promouvoir les normes les plus élevées en matière de conduite éthique et professionnelle, conformément aux valeurs et au Code de conduite de DRC, notamment en matière de prévention de l’exploitation, des abus et du harcèlement sexuels.

Le/la titulaire doit également démontrer :

- Capacité à travailler efficacement dans un environnement multiculturel ;
- Proactivité et approche coopérative et collaborative ;
- Excellentes capacités de communication, d’écoute active et de collaboration ;
- Bonnes capacités organisationnelles et attention aux détails ;
- Sens élevé de l’éthique et de l’intégrité professionnelle ;
- Capacité à travailler efficacement en équipe ;
- Flexibilité et capacité d’adaptation dans un environnement de travail dynamique.

**About us:**

The Ombuds Office for SOS Children's Villages is an independent entity within SOS Children’s Villages dedicated to supporting individuals concerned about the safety or well-being of children and young people involved in SOS programmes and services, past or present. Its approach is child-centred, participatory, and 'ground-up', meaning it adapts to each national context and seeks local solutions to local challenges.

An Ombuds is a trustworthy individual who listens, supports, and guides children, young people, and adults to find solutions to their concerns related to children and young people.

Each Ombuds follows the four principles: Confidentiality, Impartiality, Independence and Informality. They work independently of SOS Children’s Villages as a check and balance to safeguarding with the goal of finding a solution to the concern raised.

SOS Children's Villages has been in Guinea-Bissau since 1994. The first group of 51 children entered the first SOS Village, built in Guinea-Bissau, which was inaugurated on June 2, 1995, after having operated for one year on an experimental basis.

On February 1, 2001, the SOS Children's Villages Foundation of Guinea-Bissau was legally created, a Private Institution of Social Solidarity, recognized as a private-law legal entity of a foundational type., Currently, SOS Villages has 15 projects: 3 SOS Villages (Bissau, Gabú, and Canchungo, with about 243 children and youth), 2 kindergartens, 3 basic education schools, 1 Polytechnic High School (3,000 students), 2 youth training and employment centers (Sangue Novo Bissau and Canchungo), 1 Social Center, and 3 community projects: Sangue Novo (2) and Women's Empowerment, which assist about 1,200 children and 400 needy families, and 1 Family Strengthening project is underway.

**About the role:**

**Location of the role: GUINÉ-BISSAU, Bissau**

***Consultancy contract – 12 months, with the possibility of renewing based on performance and existing funds.***

In order to further their mission, SOS Children’s Villages has decided to implement an Ombuds Office.

The National Ombuds – Guiné-Bissau, as designated neutral professionals, will build awareness, promote prevention, nurture child participation, and enable children and young people to be safe in all SOS CV programs and services.

She/He is a person of trust who has the skills and interest to work through their concerns and find a local solution by:

- Adhering to the principles of confidentiality, independence, informality and impartiality;
- Guiding a fair process through listening and supporting;
- Utilizing dispute resolution skills;
- Being a check and balance;
- Identifying trends and making recommendations to Guiné-Bissau SOS Children’s Villages.

***The National Ombuds will operate independently from SOS Children’s Villages Guiné-Bissau and will report to the Regional Ombuds***.

**Profile, skills and qualifications:**

- Minimum 5 years demonstrated experience in one of the following fields: ombudsperson, mediation, child safeguarding, child protection, or child rights;
- Bachelor’s or higher degree in a relevant area such as child rights or child protection, child and youth studies, mediation, social work, child psychology, safeguarding or psychosocial wellbeing, law, or equivalent;
- Good level of knowledge of children’s rights or child protection/prevention and related issues - child safeguarding, child development and health, law, policies, regulations and International Standards related to children's care;
- Minimum 3 years demonstrated experience in working with children and youths;
- Experience in working in communities;
- Very good listener with good observational skills;
- Strong communication skills;
- Strong facilitation/training skills - Ability to develop and implement engaging trainings and ongoing support workshops for staff and children' representatives;
- Demonstrated ability to build rapport with children and young people, to identify marginalized children and young people and connect with them and build trust;
- A problem solver, adviser and mediator capable of resolving young people’s and adults’ concerns effectively and in a timely manner;
- Understanding of the local context, including laws, policies, practices, language and social norms;
- Be gender and diverse sensitive and representative;
- *Fluent in Crioulo and Portuguese.*
- *English skills and French are advantage*.
- Understand the foundations of the Ombuds’ work;
- You have the legal right to live and work in Guiné-Bissau as a consultant;
- Experience working in an NGO or international organization would be an asset.

***Other Qualifications based on Guiné-Bissau Context:***

- Strong understanding of the child protection system in Guinea-Bissau and relevant national legislation affecting children and young people.
- Demonstrated ability to engage with traditional and community leaders while maintaining independence and impartiality.
- Familiarity with challenges affecting children and youth in Guinea-Bissau, including access to education, child labor, early marriage, gender-based violence, migration, disability inclusion, and social exclusion.
- Experience working with multicultural and multilingual communities.
- Ability to travel regularly within Guinea-Bissau.
- High level of personal integrity, discretion, and professional ethics.
- No current political office or affiliation that may compromise the perceived independence of the Ombuds function.
- Contribute to safeguarding awareness campaigns tailored to the Guinea-Bissau context
- Identify systemic protection concerns and recommend practical improvements based on local realities and community feedback

